      "problem": "What is the product of solutions of the equation $4\\cdot {5}^{x-2}={x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot {5}^{x-2} & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-2} \\\\ 4\\cdot {5}^{x-2}-{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-2} & =0 \\\\ (4-{x}^{2}){5}^{x-2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n4-{x}^{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{x-2}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $4-{x}^{2}=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2+x)(2-x)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-2,2.$\n\n- The equation ${5}^{x-2}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{x-2}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-2,2,$ and their product is $(-2)\\cdot 2=-4.$",
